{#- Quadratic solved by factoring the trinomial.

    The coefficients arrive already reduced to standard form: the blueprint's
    `derived` block computes a, b, c from the hidden factors (p x + q)(r x + s),
    so this template — like quad_eq.py — reads one source of truth and the
    equation shown can never drift from the equation solved.

    `presentation_form` decides only how that standard form is DRESSED before
    the student sees it:

      standard       a x^2 + b x + c = 0
      scaled         every coefficient multiplied by `scale`, so the student must
                     divide it out before factoring
      both_sides     `shift_b`·x + `shift_c` added to BOTH sides, so the student
                     must collect terms into standard form first
      repeated_root  the same expansion, but the two linear factors coincide
                     (the constraints pin p == r and q == s)

    `a` is always positive (p, r >= 1), so the macros only fold signs on the
    lower terms. The factorisation itself is never displayed. -#}
{%- macro poly(a, b, c) -%}
{{ '' if a == 1 else a }}x^2
{%- if b != 0 %} {{ '+' if b > 0 else '-' }} {{ '' if b in (1, -1) else (b if b > 0 else -b) }}x{% endif %}
{%- if c != 0 %} {{ '+' if c > 0 else '-' }} {{ c if c > 0 else -c }}{% endif %}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro lin(b, c) -%}
{%- if b == 0 and c == 0 %}0
{%- else -%}
{%- if b != 0 %}{{ '' if b == 1 else ('-' if b == -1 else b) }}x{% endif %}
{%- if c != 0 %}{% if b != 0 %} {{ '+' if c > 0 else '-' }} {{ c if c > 0 else -c }}{% else %}{{ c }}{% endif %}{% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- set is_square = (p == r and q == s) -%}
PROBLEM TO POSE (turn ONLY this into the word problem; keep every number exactly as written):
{% if presentation_form == 'scaled' -%}
- Equation: {{ poly(scale * a, scale * b, scale * c) }} = 0
{%- elif presentation_form == 'both_sides' -%}
- Equation: {{ poly(a, b + shift_b, c + shift_c) }} = {{ lin(shift_b, shift_c) }}
{%- else -%}
- Equation: {{ poly(a, b, c) }} = 0
{%- endif %}
- Ask the student to solve the equation by FACTORING the quadratic trinomial (разложением
  на множители), not by the discriminant formula.

=== INTERNAL (guidance for you only — never show, restate, paraphrase, number, or hint any of this to the student) ===
- Method to recall:
{%- if presentation_form == 'both_sides' %}
  first move every term to one side and collect like terms — the equation is NOT yet in
  standard form, and factoring before that step is the classic error;
{%- elif presentation_form == 'scaled' %}
  the three coefficients share a common numeric factor — take it out (or divide through by
  it) first, so the trinomial that gets factored is the small one;
{%- else %}
  the equation is already in standard form a x^2 + b x + c = 0;
{%- endif %}
  then split the middle term / find the two linear factors, set EACH factor to zero, and
  read off one root per factor.
{% if a == 1 -%}
- The leading coefficient is 1, so the pair (m, n) the student is hunting satisfies both
  m·n = c AND m + n = b. Getting the product right but the sum wrong is the usual trap.
{%- else -%}
- The leading coefficient is not 1: the factors have the shape (p x + q)(r x + s), where
  p·r must reproduce the leading coefficient, q·s the constant term, AND p·s + q·r the
  middle one. All three checks have to hold at once.
{%- endif %}
{% if is_square -%}
- The two linear factors coincide here, so the equation has ONE repeated root, not two
  distinct ones. Phrase the question so that answering with a single value is natural.
{%- else -%}
- Solving x from a factor k·x + m = 0 gives x = -m/k: the sign of the constant FLIPS.
- The two roots are distinct, and both are exact rationals — they never need rounding.
{%- endif %}
- The equation must be posed exactly in the form given above. Never show the factored
  form, never name the factors, and never mention that the coefficients were built by
  expanding a product.
- Render all math as LaTeX inside $...$.
- Do NOT state or hint at the answer: the factorisation, the roots, and the number of
  roots must NOT appear in the problem text.
